    {
      "id": "ports-and-handles-as-authored-interface-geometry",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Projection Geometry",
      "position": {
        "x": 820,
        "y": 160
      },
      "width": 650,
      "height": 540,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ports-and-handles-as-authored-interface"
        },
        "markdown": "# Circular interface geometry\n\nLet the Title position be the center `C`. Let the Declaration position be `D`.\n\n`rootDirection = normalize(D - C)`\n\n`radius = length(D - C)`\n\nThe Declaration is therefore the root point on the authored interaction boundary. For a Port or Handle primitive at position `Q`:\n\n`direction = normalize(Q - C)`\n\n`boundaryPoint = C + radius * direction`\n\nOnly the primitive's direction from the Title determines its projected endpoint position. Its authored distance from the Title may be chosen for graph readability.\n\n## Layout domains\n\n- **Boundary participants:** Declaration, Ports, and Handles. Their directions are functional and protected from ordinary auto-layout.\n- **Center:** Title. Root navigation focuses it.\n- **Interior participants:** portals, documentation, examples, implementation nodes, and other presentation content. Auto-layout may arrange these within the circle.\n- **Whole frame:** may translate or scale as a unit without changing normalized endpoint directions.\n\nThe circular frame is shape-independent. At runtime, each normalized direction may be intersected with a rectangle, rounded card, circle, SVG silhouette, or another rendered node boundary.\n\n## Three dimensions\n\nReplace 2D vectors with 3D vectors. Title remains center, the Title-to-Declaration distance establishes the radius, Ports and Handles project to the sphere, and content arranges within the enclosed ball. A global up vector or later orientation anchor may be used for camera and label orientation, but endpoint projection needs only the center and each primitive's direction."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ports-and-handles-as-authored-interface-interface-map",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Authored Interface Map",
      "position": {
        "x": 820,
        "y": -500
      },
      "width": 650,
      "height": 500,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ports-and-handles-as-authored-interface"
        },
        "markdown": "# Authored node interface\n\n```text\n                         [Handle]\n                            *\n                    .---------------.\n                Port*                 *Handle\n                  /                     \\\n                 |   Portal   Content   |\n                 |      o       o       |\n                 |        [Title]       |\n                 |           O          |\n                  \\                     /\n                   *-------------------'\n              [Declaration: root Port]\n```\n\n- **Title** is the center and root focus target.\n- **Declaration** is the durable root Port and establishes the root boundary direction and radius.\n- A plain **Port** receives compatible connections.\n- A **Handle** is a Port that can initiate interaction and may declare optional actions.\n- Ports and Handles project to the circular boundary from their directions relative to Title.\n- Portals consume surfaces but remain ordinary movable interior content.\n- **Handle -> Port** is the canonical connection direction.\n- Relationship meaning remains on the edge or edge class.\n- In three dimensions the same authored boundary becomes a sphere."
      }
    },
